2879-### 46.4 Rules (frozen)
2880-
2881-1. **No fixture requires credentials or network.** A provider contract test runs from
2882-   `raw_response.json`; recording is a one-time human act, never part of the test.
2883-2. **Recording is sanitized at record time**: no API keys, no absolute paths, no wall-clock
2884-   timestamps, no account identifiers. A fixture-sanitization validator runs over `request.json`,
2885-   `raw_response.json`, and `expected_result.json` in CI. The stricter §36 egress validator runs over
2886-   `expected_result.json` and actual `ProviderResult` values, not raw provider responses: a raw
2887-   Storyboard/Animator response may need a synthetic remote URL to exercise the code that must remove
2888-   it before egress.
2889-3. **Live tests stay gated** behind `pytest.ini:3` / `STS_LIVE=1` and are never the only coverage
2890-   of a boundary. Every provider in §46.3 has an offline fixture even where the live credential
2891-   works.
2892-4. **Fixture drift is mechanical.** Provider fixtures get the same manifest-with-SHA-256 treatment
2893-   as the port fixtures (§10), so an accidental edit fails a test rather than silently changing
2894-   the meaning of every provider test.
2895-5. **The three unusable live providers** (WaveSpeed 401, retired n8n webhook, human-driven
2896-   `grok_automa` — §17.4) are exactly why 46.3 is mandatory: for these, the offline fixture is the
2897-   *only* possible verification, and their migration steps cannot be gated on live access.
2898-
2899-## 47. Contract-vs-code review — the deliberate shims
2900-
2901-Reviewing §19–§46 against the shipped code, five obligations cannot be met without a deliberate
2902-compatibility shim or an acknowledged behavior change. Each is accepted here rather than
2903-discovered mid-implementation.
2904-
2905-| # | obligation | why the code cannot meet it cleanly | frozen decision | owner |
2906-|---|---|---|---|---|
2907-| C1 | §22.6 — env vars are read-time fallbacks that never change a selection | `_seed_from_env` flips TTS to `inworld` when `INWORLD_API_KEY` is set (`settings_manager.py:85-88`) and this machine's `settings.json` already records `"selected_provider": "inworld"` | the *existing* file is left alone (it is now an explicit user choice); only future seeding stops flipping. Fresh installs behave differently from today — accepted | 11.3 |
2908-| C2 | §24.1 rule 3 — `settings.json` is authoritative for every domain | `animation_routes.py:194` never reads `domains.animator.selected_provider` (D15/§24.1) | wiring it changes what an existing animator run selects when the modal and settings disagree. Accepted, and 14.3 must state the resulting behavior in its record | 14.3 |
2909-| C3 | §32.4 / §36 L9 — remote URLs never cross into results | `image_url` is persisted inside `storyboard.json` (`storyboard/routes.py:233`) and re-read by `adapters/storyboard.py:46`, so old manifests on disk contain them | reading an old manifest strips the field at load; the *file* is not rewritten. A shim, deliberately, because rewriting user projects to satisfy an egress rule is worse than filtering on read | 14.2 |
2910-| C4 | §22.6 — request options override stored settings | `_kie_ai_options.update(provider_settings)` inverts precedence for Kie AI only (§40.2 O4) | corrected to request-wins for all providers. A behavior change for anyone relying on the inversion; no evidence anyone does | 14.3 |
2911-| C5 | §24.1 — an explicit legacy request provider wins over stored settings | the bulk Storyboard route ignores its accepted-extra `provider` field and reads only `provider_override`; the pipeline sends only `provider` (`services.py:563`) | 14.2 normalizes `provider_override` first, then the legacy `provider` alias, then stored settings. Activating the previously dead legacy field is an intentional behavior correction when it disagrees with settings | 14.2 |
